The best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's a battle-tested library that already wraps `File.*`, `Directory.*`, and more.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

// File.ReadAllText
Assert.AreEqual("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Load("/data/config.json"));

// Directory.Exists
Assert.IsTrue(service.DirectoryExists("/data"));

// File.WriteAllText
service.Save("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.AreEqual("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
```

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the entire `System.IO` surface, ships with `MockFileSystem` for testing, and avoids you maintaining your own interface. Once injected, all three of your statics (`File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, `Directory.Exists`) are testable through the single `IFileSystem` dependency.

